Package: montech-hyperflow
Version: @VERSION@
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Maintainer: banguit <banguit@users.noreply.github.com>
Homepage: https://github.com/banguit/montech-hyperflow-linux
Depends: python3 (>= 3.8), adduser, udev
Recommends: systemd, python3-gi, gir1.2-gtk-3.0,
 gir1.2-ayatanaappindicator3-0.1, polkitd | policykit-1
Suggests: gnome-shell-extension-appindicator
Provides: montech-hyperflow-tray
Conflicts: montech-hyperflow-tray
Replaces: montech-hyperflow-tray
Description: unofficial Linux driver for the Montech HyperFlow AIO pump display
 Drives the 7-segment temperature display on the pump head of a Montech
 HyperFlow Digital 241.0.0 all-in-one liquid cooler. A small daemon reads a
 hwmon sensor -- the CPU package by default, optionally the GPU -- and writes
 the value to the pump head over hidraw once a second. A udev rule, a systemd
 service, a suspend/resume hook and an optional panel indicator are included.
 .
 The daemon is pure Python standard library: no hidapi, no libusb, no pyudev,
 no D-Bus binding and no kernel module.
 .
 UNOFFICIAL COMMUNITY SOFTWARE. This is not a Montech product. It is not
 produced, endorsed, supported, reviewed or approved by Montech, by TCOMAS or
 by SEMICO, and it contains no vendor code, firmware, strings or artwork. The
 wire format was documented independently, by reverse engineering for
 interoperability, so that the hardware works on Linux. It comes with no
 warranty whatsoever; see the GPL, sections 15 and 16.
 .
 Target hardware: USB ID 1a2c:4e85, OEM platform TCOMAS DH-C100. Rebrands of
 the same pump head sold under other names are very likely to work unchanged.
 .
 WHY THE GUI LIBRARIES ARE RECOMMENDS AND NOT DEPENDS. This package carries
 both the daemon and the optional panel indicator, but nothing outside
 montech-hyperflow-tray imports GTK; the daemon runs happily on a machine with
 no display server at all. Listing PyGObject, GTK 3, AyatanaAppIndicator and
 polkit under Depends would force a headless server or a NAS to pull in a
 desktop stack it will never run, so they are Recommends: the default
 apt-get install pulls them in and the indicator works, while
 "apt-get --no-install-recommends install montech-hyperflow" gets the driver
 alone. Only the indicator breaks if they are absent, and only when it is run.
 .
 Installing this package does not enable the service. Check that
 "montech-hyperflow --list" finds the device and reports it writable, then
 turn it on with "systemctl enable --now montech-hyperflow.service".
